@meta
id: 31-09
title: Scenario — four things wrong in init.lua
chapter: 31
type: scenario
skills: lua-config, nvim-differences, obj-word, search-slash
par: 140
minutes: 12
vscode: reading a stack trace in the output panel and guessing
open: init.lua

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    NVIM-12  A colleague hand-ported their vimrc over the weekend. Neovim
             starts, complains, and half the config does not take effect.
             Four separate problems. Fix the file.

This is what they reported, in the order they hit it:

  1. Startup prints an error from the options section: something about a
     string being expected. One option is being set through the wrong
     table for its type.

  2. `<leader>w` works, but `:map <leader>w` shows it with no description
     and the which-key popup lists it as unnamed. Every other mapping in
     the file is fine. Give it the description `Write the file`.

  3. The plugin manager warns about an invalid field in the telescope
     spec. The field is real — it is just the wrong plugin manager's
     spelling. lazy.nvim wants the other one.

  4. `:checkhealth` reports a deprecated function call in the status line
     helper. The replacement dropped the `buf_` prefix when the function
     stopped being buffer-specific.

Fix all four and change nothing else. The file is graded in vim, and the
Lua is never run — read it the way you would read a diff.

@keys
| `ciw` | change the word under the cursor |
| `/text` `Enter` | search to navigate, rather than counting lines |
| `$` `i` | insert just before the last character on the line |

@hints
All four fixes are on four different lines and none of them needs more
than one small edit. Search for each symptom rather than scrolling.
--
Problem 1: `vim.o` takes plain values, `vim.opt` takes tables and has
`:append`. A list option assigned a Lua table has to go through `vim.opt`.
Problem 4: `vim.lsp.buf_get_clients()` became `vim.lsp.get_clients()` in
0.10 and is on its way out.
--
Problem 2 is a missing fourth argument: the options table goes after the
right-hand side, inside the closing paren. `$` lands on the `)`, and `i`
inserts before it. Problem 3: packer called it `requires`; lazy.nvim calls
it `dependencies`.

@solution
    /\.o\.wild<Enter> l ciw opt <Esc>
    /leader>w<Enter> $ i, { desc = 'Write the file' } <Esc>
    /requires<Enter> ciw dependencies <Esc>
    /buf_get<Enter> ciw get_clients <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

About 120 keystrokes, and four searches doing all the navigation.

Each of these is a mistake people actually make, and each one fails in a
different and instructive way:

- **`vim.o` with a table** is a hard error at startup, which sounds worse
  than it is. The whole file after the failing line is skipped, so one bad
  line looks like "none of my config loaded". When Neovim starts looking
  like stock vim, read the first error, not the last.
- **A keymap with no `desc`** fails silently and forever. Nothing breaks;
  the mapping is simply undiscoverable. This is why the drill made you type
  it every rep.
- **`requires` vs `dependencies`** is the tax on a plugin ecosystem that
  moved from packer to lazy.nvim. Copied snippets from a 2021 blog post are
  the usual source, and the only defence is reading the plugin manager's
  own README rather than the first search result.
- **Deprecated API** is the running cost of Neovim's pace. Functions get
  renamed, the old name keeps working for a release or two with a warning,
  and `:checkhealth` tells you before it breaks. Vim does not have this
  problem, because vim does not move. That is a genuine trade-off and you
  should count it on the honest side of the ledger, not the marketing side.

@start init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua

vim.g.mapleader = ' '

-- Options
vim.opt.number = true
vim.opt.expandtab = true
vim.o.wildmode = { 'longest', 'full' }
vim.opt.scrolloff = 8

-- Mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>w', ':w<CR>')

-- Plugins
require('lazy').setup({
  {
    'nvim-telescope/telescope.nvim',
    requires = { 'nvim-lua/plenary.nvim' },
  },
})

-- Status line helper
local function lsp_attached()
  return #vim.lsp.buf_get_clients() > 0
end

@expected init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua

vim.g.mapleader = ' '

-- Options
vim.opt.number = true
vim.opt.expandtab = true
vim.opt.wildmode = { 'longest', 'full' }
vim.opt.scrolloff = 8

-- Mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>w', ':w<CR>', { desc = 'Write the file' })

-- Plugins
require('lazy').setup({
  {
    'nvim-telescope/telescope.nvim',
    dependencies = { 'nvim-lua/plenary.nvim' },
  },
})

-- Status line helper
local function lsp_attached()
  return #vim.lsp.get_clients() > 0
end
